        "content": "[Spring AI](https://spring.io/projects/spring-ai) is a Spring ecosystem project for building Java applications that integrate with generative AI models.

### Key capabilities

- **Portable model APIs** for chat, text, image, audio, and embedding models
- **Multiple providers**, including OpenAI, Anthropic, Azure OpenAI, Amazon Bedrock, Google Vertex AI, and local models through Ollama
- **`ChatClient`**, a fluent API for prompts, model calls, structured responses, and streaming
- **Structured output**, mapping model responses to Java types
- **Tool calling**, allowing models to invoke application-defined Java functions
- **RAG support**, including document ingestion, text splitting, embedding, retrieval, and prompt augmentation
- **Vector-store integrations** for databases such as PostgreSQL/PGvector, Redis, MongoDB Atlas, Elasticsearch, and various dedicated vector databases
- **Conversation memory**
- **Observability and metrics** through Spring Boot and Micrometer
- **Spring Boot auto-configuration and starters**

A simple example:

```java
@RestController
class AiController {

    private final ChatClient chatClient;

    AiController(ChatClient.Builder builder) {
        this.chatClient = builder.build();
    }

    @GetMapping("/ask")
    String ask(@RequestParam String question) {
        return chatClient.prompt()
                .user(question)
                .call()
                .content();
    }
}
```

You would also add the appropriate model-provider starter and configure its credentials—for example, an OpenAI API key—in Spring Boot configuration.

Spring AI is especially useful when you want to build AI features while retaining familiar Spring patterns such as dependency injection, auto-configuration, configuration properties, testing, and observability.

Official resources:

- [Spring AI project page](https://spring.io/projects/spring-ai)
- [Spring AI reference documentation](https://docs.spring.io/spring-ai/reference/)
